- op: discuss  # d1e2
  at: '2026-08-18T06:23:20Z'  # d1e2
  by: agent  # d1e2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d1e2
  clock: 3  # d1e2
  nonce: d1e2  # d1e2
  message: "DONE via PR #407 (dev 961c4c00f9). Behavioral evidence: the same script,\nbefore and after, on the same tree.\n\n  BEFORE  ./scripts/release-check\n          31 lines. Output ends mid-section at \"  Running pip-audit...\".\n          exit 1. No findings, no verdict, no summary.\n          Sections reached: 1, 2, 2b, 3(partial).\n\n  AFTER   ./scripts/release-check\n          135 lines. The six findings printed in full.\n          Sections reached: 1, 2, 2b, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, Summary -- ALL of them.\n          Section 3 verdict: \"No known vulnerabilities in dependencies\".\n\nTHE FIX: `... ) || AUDIT_EXIT=$?` with `AUDIT_EXIT=0` initialized above it. The\ninitializer is load-bearing and its absence fails ONLY on the passing path: on a\nclean audit the `||` branch never runs, so `set -u` would abort at\n`[ $AUDIT_EXIT -eq 0 ]`. My own harness caught that mid-development, which is\nwhy the test's block extraction is anchored on the initializer -- deleting it\nnow raises from the extractor rather than silently narrowing coverage.\n\nSTRUCTURAL SWEEP, since a bug in one script is a question about the rest. Every\nexit-code capture in scripts/ and .githooks/ was checked: all the others already\nuse `cmd || var=$?`, or sit in an `if` condition where set -e does not apply\n(auto-pr:1765). release-check:251 was the only instance. NOT a class.\n\nTESTS EXECUTE THE SHIPPED BLOCK (tests/test_release_check_audit_reporting.py, 6\npassing) rather than grepping it: extracted from the real script, run under real\n`set -euo pipefail` against a stub pip-audit at exits 1/2/127, asserting both\nthat findings print and that a later marker still runs. The load-bearing one is\nthe DIFFERENTIAL control, which reconstructs the pre-fix form and proves the\nharness separates them -- without an input that distinguishes the two rules, the\nother assertions could pass for reasons unrelated to the fix.\n\nADJACENT WORK TAKEN because a reporting gate that reports blocks the release it\ngates: the six now-visible CVEs are PINNED away, not ignored, per the script's\nown policy -- cryptography>=50.0.0, nltk>=3.10.0, and pyopenssl>=26.4.0 (needed\nfor cryptography 50 to resolve; CI already ships it). Verified via\nimportlib.metadata rather than assumed: NEITHER package is a declared dependency\nof any shipped hypergumbo package (cryptography arrives via Authlib/py-webauthn,\nnltk via nothing), so no released artifact was ever affected.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IMMEDIATELY SURFACED, and the causal chain is exact: sections 3\n(secrets scan) and 4 (lint) had not run in an unknown length of time, and the\npre-commit hook lints only `packages/*/src packages/*/tests` -- so top-level\ntests/ is covered by NOTHING except release-check section 4. Result: 4 ruff\nerrors accumulated in tests/ (3 auto-fixable RUF100, 1 S103), and gitleaks\nreports 4 findings, all triaged as FALSE POSITIVES (one is the kwarg\n`prohibited_sink_zone=\"host_fs\"`; two are fixtures belonging to the\nsecret-SCRUBBING tests, which cannot exist without sample secrets; one is an INI\nfixture beside `base_url = https://api.example.com`). Left for the owner to\nauthorise, since allowlisting security findings is not an agent call.\n\nUNRESOLVED, recorded rather than dropped: one run emitted\n\"release-check: line 355: g.: command not found\" immediately after a section-5\nfailure. `bash -n` is clean, there is no eval, no such token exists in the file,\nand it did NOT recur on the passing run. Not reproducible on demand."  # d1e2
